  "prediction": "**Groundbreaking Surgery Gives Boy Born Without Ears a New Lease on Life**\n\nNine-year-old Kieran Sorkin, born without ears and deaf, has undergone a pioneering surgery at Great Ormond Street Hospital in London. The procedure, which involved creating a pair of ears from Kieran's own ribs, has given him a new sense of confidence and self-esteem.\n\nKieran, from Hertfordshire, was born with bilateral microtia, a rare condition where the external ear is underdeveloped. He was also deaf, but thanks to previous operations and a hearing aid, he can now hear. However, he had always wanted to look like his friends and have the ability to wear sunglasses and earphones.\n\nThe surgery, led by consultant plastic surgeon Neil Bulstrode, involved harvesting cartilage from Kieran's ribs and shaping it into frameworks for his ears. The frameworks were then grafted onto Kieran's head, and the skin was shaped to the contours of the new ears using a vacuum.\n\nThe surgery is primarily cosmetic, but it brings huge psychological benefits, according to Mr. Bulstrode. \"If you can change the confidence of a patient at this young age, you can change their whole trajectory in life,\" he said.\n\nKieran's reaction to his new ears was immediate and positive. \"Wow!\" he exclaimed, before starting to giggle. However, the operation on his ribs means it hurts when he laughs.\n\nThe surgery is not without its challenges, and Kieran will need a second operation in six months to make his ears stand out from the scalp more. However, he is already delighted with the result, and his parents are overjoyed.\n\nThe procedure is a major operation, but it brings a significant improvement in quality of life for children with microtia. Their confidence improves exponentially, and their performance at school improves. As Mr. Bulstrode said, \"If you can improve a young person's confidence, you can alter their whole trajectory in life.\"\n\nResearchers at the University College London Institute for Child Health are working on a new approach to ear reconstruction using stem cells from a child's own fat tissue. This approach would be far less invasive than the current treatment and could potentially revolutionize the field of reconstructive surgery.\n\nFor now, Kieran's story is a testament to the power of medical innovation and the importance of giving children the confidence to succeed. As his mother, Louise, said, \"I just want him to be accepted like everyone else.\"",
